  "generated_text": "1. The circumference of the circle is $2\\pi \\times 6 = 12\\pi$ cm.\n2. Each sector of the circle has an arc length of $\\frac{12\\pi}{3} = 4\\pi$ cm.\n3. When a sector is rolled into a cone, the arc length of the sector becomes the circumference of the base of the cone.\n4. The height of the cone is the radius of the circle formed by the arc length.\n5. The radius of the base of the cone is $6$ cm.\n6. The height of the cone can be found using the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the radius, the height, and the slant height of the cone.\n\nLet $h$ be the height of the cone.\n\\[\n6^2 + h^2 = 6^2\n\\]\n\\[\n36 + h^2 = 36\n\\]\n\\[\nh^2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nh = 0\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone that can be created by rolling one of the sectors of the circle is $0$ cm.\n\n\\boxed{0}",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[0, '0']",
    "gold_answer": "The circumference of the whole circle is $2 \\pi \\cdot 6 = 12 \\pi$, so the circumference of the base of the cone is $12 \\pi/3 = 4 \\pi$.  Hence, the radius of the base of the cone is $4 \\pi/(2 \\pi) = 2$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(2 cm);\n\nfill((0,0)--arc((0,0),1,-60,60)--cycle,gray(0.7));\ndraw(Circle((0,0),1));\ndraw((0,0)--dir(60));\ndraw((0,0)--dir(180));\ndraw((0,0)--dir(300));\nlabel(\"$6$\", dir(60)/2, NW);\n[/asy]\n\nThe slant height of the cone is 6 (the radius of the original circle), so by Pythagoras, the height of the cone is $\\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{32} = \\boxed{4 \\sqrt{2}}$.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.8 cm);\n\ndraw((-2,0)--(2,0)--(0,4*sqrt(2))--cycle);\ndraw((0,0)--(0,4*sqrt(2)));\n\nlabel(\"$2$\", (1,0), S);\nlabel(\"$6$\", (1,2*sqrt(2)), NE);\nlabel(\"$4 \\sqrt{2}$\", (0,0.7*2*sqrt(2)), W);\n[/asy]",
    "is_correct": false
  },
